The basis of the mail daemon is that it checks for local mail,
specifically in /var/spool/mail/<usrname> (or a similar location on
non-linux systems).  Since you're already logged into the <usrname>
account, this file is already readable by any command line mail
program.  The daemon can periodically check the spool file, and when a
new message appears, it shows up and hands you a scroll of mail. 
Reading the scroll essentially executes a combination of ! (escape to
shell) and MAILREADER (defined in unixconf.h).

To check for web-based email, you'd have to hack in an entire module
that implemented some http calls to the server of choice (which could
be defined in defaults.nh), manage the login/password in a secure
manner, check for unread messages.  Reading the scroll would need to
repeat the process to get the text of the message, then create an
ncurses (or similar) window in nethack to display the text (preferably
stripped of the html bits).

I don't know how MAPI-enabled clients like Outlook work, but in that
case, the mail is stored somewhere on your system.  The daemon code
could be rewritten to periodically check the status of that file
instead of /var/spool/mail/<usrname>.  Then, it seems like it should
work ok.  The big difference is that, in windows, each email client
puts messages in a different place.  In the unix world, your mail
server determines this, and /var/spool/mail seems to be fairly
standard.

